Since node \(R\) is directly connected to ground, the circuit can be redrawn as: <img src="img/777be08285e4ff2f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,307px)"> The capacitors connected from node \(B\) to ground are in parallel, giving \[C_B=C_s+C_c.\] Similarly, the capacitors connected from node \(Y\) to ground are also in parallel, giving \[C_Y=C_s+C_c.\] The series equivalent through ground is \[C_{\text{series}}
=
\frac{C_BC_Y}{C_B+C_Y}
=
\frac{(C_s+C_c)^2}{2(C_s+C_c)}
=
\frac{C_s+C_c}{2}.\] Since this branch is in parallel with the capacitor \(C_c\), \[C_{BY}
=
C_c
+
C_{\text{series}}
=
C_c+\frac{C_s+C_c}{2}.\] Therefore, \[C_{BY}
=
\frac{2C_c+C_s+C_c}{2}
=
\frac{C_s+3C_c}{2}.\] Hence, \[\boxed{\textbf{C. }\frac{C_s+3C_c}{2}}.\]

For a half-wave rectified symmetrical square wave, \[i(t)=
\begin{cases}
2\,\text{A}, &amp; 0\le t&lt;\dfrac{T}{2},\\ 0, &amp; \dfrac{T}{2}\le t&lt;T.
\end{cases}\] The RMS value is \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}i^2(t)\,dt}.\] Substituting the waveform, \[I_{\mathrm{RMS}}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\left[\int_{0}^{T/2}(2)^2\,dt\right]}
=\sqrt{\frac{4(T/2)}{T}}
=\sqrt{2}\,\text{A}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\sqrt{2}\,\text{A}}\]

Two long parallel wires carry equal current \(I\) in the same direction and are separated by a distance \(d\). The magnetic field intensity due to a long straight conductor at a distance \(r\) is \[H=\frac{I}{2\pi r}\] Consider a point on the line midway between the two conductors. The distance of this point from each wire is \[r=\frac{d}{2}\] Hence, the magnitudes of the magnetic fields produced by the two wires are equal: \[H_1=H_2=\frac{I}{2\pi(d/2)}
=\frac{I}{\pi d}\] Using the right-hand thumb rule, the magnetic fields at the midpoint are in opposite directions. Therefore, \[\vec{H}_{\text{net}}
=
\vec{H}_1+\vec{H}_2
=
0\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{B}\]

Let the per-phase winding impedance be \(Z\). <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> When all windings are reconnected as a single-phase equivalent circuit, all windings are connected in additive polarity for maximum voltage. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> Current rating of a winding remains unchanged as long as the cross-sectional area of the conductor remains constant. For single-phase connection, \[V_{x}=3V_{1}\] KVA rating in delta connection: \[Q_{\Delta}=3V_{1}I\] KVA rating in equivalent single-phase connection: \[Q_{\text{single}}
=
(3V_{1})I\] \[Q_{\text{single}}
=
Q_{\Delta}\] Hence KVA rating remains unchanged. Therefore voltage ratio becomes \[\frac{3V_{1}}{3V_{2}}\] which is equal to the original turns ratio. If windings are connected in parallel, voltage rating remains unchanged but current rating becomes three times, therefore KVA rating still remains the same. Hence none of the given options satisfy the required condition.

