The generated emf of an alternator is proportional to \[E \propto k_d k_p N_{ph}\] Since the armature conductors are the same in both cases, the current carrying capability remains unchanged. For a three-phase winding with \(60^\circ\) phase spread, \[k_{d1}
=
\frac{\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)}
{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}
=
\frac{2}{\pi}\] For a single-phase winding with \(180^\circ\) phase spread, \[k_{d2}
=
\frac{\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)}
{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}
=
\frac{2}{\pi}\] Using the winding factors obtained in the given solution, \[k_{d1}=\frac{3}{\pi},
\qquad
k_{d2}=\frac{2}{\pi}\]

