In a dual-slope ADC: <ul><li>The unknown input voltage \(V_{in}\) is integrated for a fixed time \(T_1\).</li><li>A known reference voltage \(V_{ref}\) of opposite polarity is then applied.</li><li>The time taken for the integrator output to return to zero is the deintegration time \(T_2\).</li></ul> For a dual-slope ADC, \[\frac{V_{in}}{V_{ref}}
=
\frac{T_2}{T_1}\] Hence, \[V_{in}
=
V_{ref}\frac{T_2}{T_1}\] Given, \[V_{ref}=100\,\text{mV}\] \[T_1=300\,\text{ms}, \qquad T_2=370.2\,\text{ms}\] Therefore, \[V_{in}
=
100\times\frac{370.2}{300}\] \[V_{in}
=
123.4\,\text{mV}\] A \(3\frac12\)-digit DVM can display values up to 199.9 \[\boxed{\textbf{A. }123.4}\]
